{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-343255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{y}^{″}+(3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)){y}^{′}+(x+1)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-3x)W(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f2134f9d1dd8d88f385dff3c82df3dba46b6703acd8330ae8aa65574813ae536"} {"question" "q-343255" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f6