ect "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343293" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$"] ["text" "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x>0$"] ["text" "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x>0$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343293" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(\\pi )=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(\\pi )=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(\\pi )=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\pi ) & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f2134f9d1dd8d88f385dff3c82df3dba46b6703acd8330ae8aa65574813ae536"} {"question" "q-343294" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35