badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0} \\\\ 0 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f2134f9d1dd8d88f385dff3c82df3dba46b6703acd8330ae8aa65574813ae536"} {"question" "q-343295" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-343295" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "