ete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "q-342724" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/state.json" "file_sha256" "69c5caa3276eac16badd5b9687f619f7626c9b6ffe63853dd701b59cceb35bb5"} {"question" "e-21868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/example-21868.json" "file_sha256" "20f6089e40d564c40be10b291d482b8e052a91e6c0c29841f3be805d43f19008"} {"question" "e-21868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/example-21868.json" "file_sha256" "20f6089e40d564c40be10b291d482b8e052a91e6c0c29841f3be805d43f19008"} {"question" "e-21869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/example-21869.json" "file_sha256" "62b553df0fbf84c694252ff35fbd1dbf24a85df64f5aad30e4bb8da3ae5b998a"} {"question" "e-21869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070717/example-21869.json" "file_sha256" "62b553df0fbf84c694252ff35fbd1dbf24a85df64f5aad